<div class="sec"><div style="text-align:center;max-width:780px;margin:0 auto"><span class="k">Self-hosting, asked</span><h2 class="h2" style="margin-top:12px">Questions about owning it.</h2></div><div class="fql2"><details class="fq2" open><summary><h3>Who runs it?</h3><span class="pm"></span></summary><p>You do. On a VPS, a home server or any Linux or macOS machine with Docker. One image, one volume.</p></details><details class="fq2"><summary><h3>Where is my data?</h3><span class="pm"></span></summary><p>On that machine, in one SQLite file. Back it up with one command.</p></details><details class="fq2"><summary><h3>Do I need an account?</h3><span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Not with us. You set an admin login on your own Core.</p></details><details class="fq2"><summary><h3>What if the project stops?</h3><span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Your copy keeps running, and the data is a plain SQLite file you can open any time.</p></details><details class="fq2"><summary><h3>Can it watch my home network?</h3><span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Yes. The agent reports to your Core over your LAN or a private network such as Tailscale. Nothing needs to be exposed to the internet.</p></details><details class="fq2"><summary><h3>How do I update it?</h3><span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Pull the new image and recreate the services. Core copies the database before it migrates, so you can roll back.</p></details><details class="fq2"><summary><h3>What can it watch?</h3><span class="pm"></span></summary><p>HTTP and TCP targets, cron heartbeats, and Linux or macOS servers, including their services and containers.</p></details><details class="fq2"><summary><h3>Can my apps send logs?</h3><span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Yes. Post JSON with curl, or point any OpenTelemetry exporter at it. Add an ingest key per app.</p></details></div></div>
